Level 4 introduced the decoy. Here we go deeper into the most common kind: **attraction** — a sacrifice that **forces the enemy king** onto a bad square.

The king usually has to take the piece (or it is check), so you decide where it stands next move. Good squares to pull the king to:

- a square where it can be **mated** by the next check (h7 on an open h-file, a2 in front of a pawn storm);
- a square where it is **forked** with another piece;
- a square on the **line** of your queen, rook or bishop.

Before you sacrifice, play the position **after the king takes** in your head and ask: **what is my next check, and where can the king go?**

Most tactics are **forcing**: checks, captures, threats. But some of the strongest moves are **quiet** — no check, no capture — and still win, because they create a threat the opponent **cannot meet**.

Typical quiet moves:

- **lining up** a battery (bishop behind queen, rooks doubled) so a mate threat appears;
- **stepping aside** with a piece so another piece can use its square;
- **bringing a knight closer** to support a mate.

Why this matters: at 1000–1500 players only look at checks and captures. When there is no forcing move, ask: **"what would I do if it were my move again?"** — and find the quiet move that prepares it.

Real tactics rarely use one idea alone. A **combination** links two motifs: the first move **creates** the target, the second move **hits** it.

Common pairs:

- **attraction + fork:** a sacrifice pulls the king onto a fork square;
- **trade + fork:** an exchange brings an enemy piece onto a forkable square;
- **pin + double attack**, **discovered check + capture**, **deflection + fork**.

How to find them: if you see a tactic that *almost* works (a fork that misses by one square, a pin that is one defender short), ask **which forcing move would fix that one detail**.

The **exchange sacrifice** gives a rook (5) for a bishop or knight (3). It is the most common sacrifice in strong players' games, because a rook often has **nothing to do** while a minor piece is a key defender.

Give the exchange when:

- the minor piece you take is the **key defender** of the king or of an important square;
- it **breaks the pawn shield** in front of the king (for example ...Rxc3 against a king on c1);
- it **wins time** for a check, a pin or a fork that wins more back;
- your remaining minor piece becomes **much stronger** than the opponent's rook.

Petrosian made the positional exchange sacrifice famous; in attacks it is usually tactical: you get the material back or mate.
